        "id": 0,
        "original_id": "e416f30c-ade9-5d6a-a013-a0b91999cc9b",
        "text": "This chunk is about:\n- People: Isaak Dunayevsky, Grigori Aleksandrov, Sergey Agranyan, Mark Lisyansky\n- Roles: film composer, conductor, film director, lyricists\n- Works: Moya Moskva\n- Topics: Soviet film, operetta, film comedies\n\nFacts:\n- Isaak Osipovich Dunayevsky was born on 30 January 1900 and died on 25 July 1955.\n- Grigori Vasilyevich Aleksandrov was born on 23 January 1903 and died on 16 December 1983.\n- Isaak Dunayevsky was a Soviet film composer and conductor active in the 1930s and 1940s.\n- Isaak Dunayevsky achieved major success writing music for operettas and film comedies.\n- Isaak Dunayevsky frequently worked with Soviet film director Grigori Aleksandrov.\n- Grigori Aleksandrov was a prominent Soviet film director whose original family name was Mormonenko.\n- Grigori Aleksandrov was named People's Artist of the USSR in 1947.\n- The music for Moya Moskva was composed in 1941 by Isaak Dunayevsky.\n- The lyrics for Moya Moskva were written by Sergey Agranyan and Mark Lisyansky.\n- Grigori Aleksandrov was named a Hero of Socialist Labor in 1973.",
        "type": "TextSummary"
      },
      {
        "id": 1,
        "original_id": "58466eb8-c8e1-521b-9c95-7f9ad1f5b973",
        "text": "Page: Isaak Dunayevsky. Sentence: Isaak Osipovich Dunayevsky (Russian: \"Исаак Осипович Дунаевский\" ; also transliterated as Dunaevski or Dunaevsky; 30 January [O.S. 18 January] 1900 25 July 1955) was a Soviet film composer and conductor of the 1930s and 1940s, who achieved huge success in music for operetta and film comedies, frequently working with the film director Grigori Aleksandrov.\nPage: Grigori Aleksandrov. Sentence: Grigori Vasilyevich Aleksandrov or Alexandrov (Russian: Григо́рий Васи́льевич Алекса́ндров ; original family name was Мормоненко or Mormonenko; 23 January 1903 – 16 December 1983) was a prominent Soviet film director who was named a People's Artist of the USSR in 1947 and a Hero of Socialist Labor in 1973.\nPage: Moya Moskva. Sentence: The music was composed in 1941 by Isaak Dunayevsky and the lyrics were written by Sergey Agranyan and Mark Lisyansky.",
